VERDICT -- final
================
RESFRAC 0.05 SHIPS.  First measurement on the engine that actually ships -- the recon extensions
were rebuilt from source and all five now md5-match the submission zip's.

        A mean     B mean   by mean | A med     B med   by med | A spread | paired
  P1     566,837    571,668   +0.9% |  510,450  571,668 +12.0% |   34.2%  | +12.5 +12.0 -16.2
  P3   4,636,794  4,477,641   -3.4% |4,583,170 4,468,072 -2.5% |    3.5%  |  -2.5  -2.5  -5.2
  P27    859,349    692,860  -19.4% |  859,349  692,860 -19.4% |    0.0%  | -19.4 -19.4 -19.4
  P16  3,150,117  2,996,946   -4.9% |3,252,294 3,022,891 -7.1% |   10.4%  |  -7.1  +2.6  -9.4
  P7     877,884    757,867  -13.7% |  860,744  749,573 -12.9% |    6.0%  | -17.8 -13.2  -9.8
  P33    977,769    933,974   -4.5% |  964,466  933,974 -3.2% |    7.6%  |  -1.5  -8.5  -3.2

  18 paired cells, B wins 15.  ratio-mean -7.10%, geo-mean -7.58%, mean-sum -637,795 (-5.76%).

P1 DOES NOT LOSE, AND THE MEDIAN SAYS OTHERWISE ONLY BECAUSE OF VARIANCE.  By median B is 12.0%
behind; by MEAN it is 0.9% ahead, which is a tie.  A's three draws span 508,193 / 510,450 / 681,869
-- 34.2% -- while B returns 571,668 three times with identical components.  The submission is one
run, so A is a gamble with B's expectation and B is that expectation delivered flat.  Preferring a
34% spread because its median landed well is not a reason.

WHAT THE ENGINE CORRECTION COST THE EARLIER CLAIM.  On the stale engine wdef measured 18/18 and
-17.36%; here it is 15/18 and -7.10%.  The shipped engine had already closed roughly half the
headroom the reserve change was collecting -- on P1 at RESFRAC 0.50 it returns ~510k where the
stale engine returned ~700k.  The direction survived; the magnitude did not, and the 18/18 framing
was withdrawn before anything was submitted.

WHY THE STALE ENGINE WENT UNNOTICED FOR A WHOLE SESSION.  harness/run1.py refuses to run when the
extensions are missing or ABI-wrong -- it was written after a silent pure-Python fallback cost an
A/B study -- but it cannot detect extensions that import fine and are simply older than the .cpp
beside them.  Every experiment was internally fair, both arms sharing the stale build, so nothing
in the results looked wrong until two artifacts were compared directly.

STILL UNMEASURED: the OGC_CPUCAP change.  This container reports cpu_count() == 4, so the cap is a
no-op here and cannot be observed.  It rests on the organisers' notice that the 400% allowance is
enforced by throttling with every core visible, which makes os.cpu_count() return the host's count
and this module request seven workers against four cores.
